## Source quality and implementation cautions

The **highest-value open numeric sources** in this pass were the **CALIPER 2020 DxH 900 pediatric interval paper**, the **open Brazilian pediatric ITP guideline**, the **open Chinese pediatric ITP adaptation**, the **open ASH pediatric leukocytosis review**, the **open pediatric TTP review**, the **open pediatric DIC review**, the **open Blood Advances severe aplastic anemia consensus**, and the **open Journal of Pediatrics eosinophilia cohort**. Those sources are the main reason most rows above are marked `open-access` and `retrievable_numeric = yes`. citeturn0search0turn21search1turn25search0turn22search4turn13search1turn20search0turn16search0turn24search3

The main design caveat is that many of the CBC-interval rows are **analyzer-specific reference-interval boundaries**, not disease cutoffs. I therefore marked those rows as **`inference`**, because the source directly states the healthy pediatric RI, while the row itself converts that RI boundary into a candidate “high” or “low” interpretation trigger. That is exactly where the existing module’s **local-lab-range precedence** rule should continue to dominate. fileciteturn0file0 citeturn2search1turn7search0

## Gaps and unresolved conflicts

The biggest unresolved source gap remains **CALIPER 2023 Mindray**: it is in the supplied bundle, but its numeric tables were not independently retrievable in an open form during this pass, so I did **not** use it to populate threshold cells. Instead, I used the openly retrievable **CALIPER 2020** values. fileciteturn0file2 citeturn0search0turn7search0

I also did **not** emit a numeric pediatric **peripheral-blast referral** row. I found strong open thresholds for age-banded leukocytosis, leukemoid reaction, and hyperleukocytosis, but I did **not** find a comparably stable, openly retrievable, universally used pediatric CBC-only blast-percentage cutoff that was worth formalizing into a threshold row. That gap is better handled downstream as a **non-numeric smear alert** candidate rather than as a numeric platform-synthesis threshold.

Finally, pediatric eosinophilia has a **minor but real classification conflict** across sources: the open 2023 *Journal of Pediatrics* cohort uses **severe eosinophilia >4.5 ×10*9/L**, while another 2023 pediatric series defines severe as **≥5.0 ×10*9/L**. I retained the **open-access `>4.5 10*9/L`** numbers in the table because they were independently retrievable in full text, but that portability caveat should stay visible in downstream verification. citeturn24search3turn24search0